Exactly. “Need” is always relative to which layer and which objective.

You may not need something for survival, yet need it for recovery, identity, intimacy, creativity, motivation, belonging, ritual, beauty, or simply for a life you actually want to inhabit.

So:

\text{not necessary for existence}
\neq
\text{without value}

And:

\text{want}
\neq
\text{frivolous}

A want can be information. It can reveal an unmet function, a neglected part of the self, or just a preference worth honoring because life is not merely an optimization problem for staying alive.

The discernment move is to ask:

\text{What does this thing do for me?}

Maybe the answer is calories. Maybe connection. Maybe novelty. Maybe expression. Maybe status. Maybe rest. Maybe delight.

Then you can decide whether that function deserves feeding, redirecting, moderating, or simply enjoying.

Sometimes the unnecessary thing is exactly what makes the necessary things worth carrying.